1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.008
This video describes the technique for examining pupils.

2
00:00:04.008 --> 00:00:08.017
First, perform hand hygiene, don PPE if required.

3
00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:12.025
Introduce yourself, explain the examination and gain consent.

4
00:00:12.025 --> 00:00:19.541
Pause briefly to observe the eyes, giving particular attention to the appearance of the pupils.

5
00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:24.300
Comment on any relative difference in size, colour and shape,

6
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.060
as well as any evidence of ptosis or squint.

7
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.820
If there is any difference in pupil size known as

8
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.580
an isochoria, then an efferent problem may be present.

9
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.780
Observe in dimmer, then brighter lighting conditions to see if it accentuates any difference.

10
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.887
Next, ask the patient to fix on a distance target and examine the pupil

11
00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:51.993
responses in a dimly lit room using the brightest light setting of the arc

12
00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:52.580
light loop.

13
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.080
Now test and observe the three light responses.

14
00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.580
Start with the direct response by observing the illuminated pupil.

15
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:07.080
Then examine the consensual response by observing the other pupil that is not being directly illuminated.

16
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:14.440
Thirdly, look for a relative afferent papillary defect, often shortened to REPD, by using the

17
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.260
swinging light test.

18
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.262
The key to performing this test is to swiftly move the light between the

19
00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:22.120
two eyes before

20
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.520
the pupil has time to escape.

21
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.920
But then wait for 2-3 seconds, for the pupil to stabilise, observing carefully for any overall

22
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.720
change in size.

23
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.541
In younger people you may notice a brief period of fluctuation as the pupil

24
00:01:36.541 --> 00:01:37.360
bounces up and

25
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.545
down before settling.

26
00:01:38.545 --> 00:01:41.310
This is called hippus in as normal.

27
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.680
During the swaying light test if

28
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.715
the pupil paradoxically dilates when light is shown upon it, an RAPD is present.

29
00:01:50.715 --> 00:01:51.720
In this

30
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.621
examination the left pupil dilates when illuminated.

31
00:01:55.621 --> 00:01:58.965
A left RAPD is therefore present.

32
00:01:58.965 --> 00:02:00.080
This strongly

33
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.440
suggests that there is pathology of either the retina or optic nerve on the left side.

34
00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.649
The final test is the near response.

35
00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:12.120
Ask the patient to fix in a distance target, then ask

36
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.600
them to look at a detailed near target such as the bird on the

37
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.560
Arclight. This induces

38
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:17.985
miosis.

39
00:02:17.985 --> 00:02:20.960
You have now completed the pupil examination.

40
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.360
Thank the patient and explain and record your

41
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.840
findings, finishing by repeating hand hygiene.
